+**Parameters**
+*   $R_1, R_2, R_3$: The number of components for each mode combination. These can be set differently depending on the complexity of each mode [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+*   In the context of 3D scene modeling (e.g., TensoRF), it is common to assume equal complexity along axes, setting $R_1 = R_2 = R_3 = R$ [S1][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
+*   **Precision:** Gradient-based optimization of the factors can suffer from local minima; careful initialization (e.g., via CP) is recommended [G].
+*   **Boundaries:** The tensor represents discrete data, so trilinear interpolation is typically applied to model a continuous field during rendering [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input:** If scene complexity varies significantly across axes, setting $R_1=R_2=R_3=R$ may underfit or overfit specific dimensions; asymmetric $R$ values should be used [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
